From 0b05c1076b8d772d5855ee32caf6e2530c6bbcb7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 04:38:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 08/34] Add engine watchdog; stop tunnel watchdog masking
 backend death

On 2026-08-06 03:13:30 both vLLM engines died with a sample_tokens RPC
timeout, preceded by "no available shared memory broadcast block", with 63
requests in flight. EngineCore raised EngineDeadError and the API server shut
down, but docker still reported the container Up with the GPUs at 4 MiB. The
run then sat stalled for 2.6 hours.

The tunnel watchdog could not see this and made it worse: a dead engine and a
wedged tunnel are indistinguishable from n82 because the SSH forward accepts
the connection either way, so it spent those hours rebuilding all 24 tunnels
against a dead backend, hiding the fault.

scripts/h200/engine_watchdog.sh runs on each H200 and probes its own engine on
localhost, restarting the container after 2 misses and allowing up to 20 min
for the checkpoint to load. Container "Up" is deliberately not the health
signal; only HTTP 200 is, since the container stayed Up throughout.

scripts/china/tunnel_watchdog.sh now refuses to rebuild when 80% or more of
the tunnels fail at once, logging that the backend is the likely cause. n82
has no ssh access to the H200s so it cannot fix engines, but it must not mask
them.

scripts/ops/watchdog_smoketest.sh covers 16 cases: healthy, single wedge,
46/79/83/100% failure, both real outages, debounce, container-Up-but-dead.
Verified live: both engines were dead and the watchdog recovered them.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_017wiESxhziDrnsLnG9aizTd
